## C. SSRN Paper Deep Analysis

The SSRN paper proposes a strictly mathematical/physics-based approach for Virtual Temperature Sensing (VTS) in second-life batteries.

### Problem Definition
Predicting real-time temperatures at unmonitored locations within a sealed EV battery pack repurposed for second-life grid storage, where adding physical sensors is economically unviable.

### Inputs
- Measured temperature of an active internal cell ($T_z$).
- Measured ambient temperature ($T_\infty$).
- Operating time / Pack Power (to determine heat generation rates).

### Target
- Temperature of an unmonitored corner cell ($T_x$).

### Methodology / Architecture
- **Simplified Lumped Thermal Model**: Uses recursive finite-difference equations based on fundamental energy balances (Newton's Law of Cooling, Joule heating, thermal conduction).
- **Parameters**: Relies on 7 empirically determined constant parameters (e.g., mass ratios, convection/radiation coefficients, thermal time constants).
- **Optimization**: Uses the Generalized Reduced Gradient (GRG) Nonlinear solving method (via Microsoft Excel Solver) to fit the 7 parameters to experimental training data.

### Validation
Validated against a 2019 Tesla Model 3 SR+ battery pack under constant power discharges (4h, 2h, 1h rates). Showed inherent "error decay" (initial bad guesses converge to true temperatures rapidly due to the physics equations).

